W: 1. As I was a walking one morning in Spring,
W: For to hear the birds whistle and the nightingales sing;
W: I saw a young damsel, so sweetly sang she:
W: Down by the Green Bushes he thinks to meet me.
W:
W: 2. I stepped up to her and thus I did say:
W: Why wait you my fair one, so long by the way?
W: My true Love, my true Love, so sweetly sang she,
W: Down by the Green Bushes he thinks to meet me.
W:
W: 3. I'll buy you fine beavers and a fine silken gown,
W: I will buy you fine petticoats with the flounce to the ground,
W: If you will prove loyal and constant to me
W: And forsake you own true Love, I'll be married to thee.
W:
W: 4. I want none of your petticoats and your fine silken shows:
W: I never was so poor as to marry for clothes;
W: But if you will prove loyal and constant to me
W: I'll forsake my own true Love and get married to thee.
W:
W: 5. Come let us be going, kind sir, if you please;
W: Come let us be going from beneath the green trees.
W: For my true Love is coming down yonder I see,
W: Down by the Green Bushes, where he thinks to meet me.
W:
W: 6. And when he came there and he found she was gone,
W: He stood like some lambkin, forever undone;
W: She has gone with some other, and forsaken me,
W: So adieu to Green Bushes forever, cried he.
